    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests pass, no failures/errors).\n\n## Summary\n\nI converted owner deletion to a soft-delete. Changes:\n\n**Domain model** \u2014 `Owner.java`: added a `deleted` boolean column (`@Column(name = \"deleted\")`), defaulting to `false`, with getter/setter. A newly built owner is therefore `deleted=false`.\n\n**Delete endpoint behavior** \u2014 `DeleteOwner.java`: instead of `ownerRepository.delete(owner)`, it now sets `owner.setDeleted(true)` and `save`s the owner, so the row is retained and marked deleted. The DELETE pipeline still returns 204 (2xx), and `GET /api/owners/{id}` (via `LoadOwner`'s `findById`) still finds the retained row and now reports `deleted: true`.\n\n**Create-time checks ignore deleted owners**:\n- `CheckUniqueIdentity.java`: skips any existing owner flagged deleted, so a soft-deleted owner no longer triggers the 409 household-duplicate rejection.\n- `CheckPossibleDuplicate.java`: skips deleted owners so they're not flagged as soft duplicate matches either.\n\n**API contract** \u2014 `openapi.yml`: added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the Owner response schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries the field and MapStruct maps it by name.\n\n**Schemas** \u2014 added a `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE` column to all four DB schemas (hsqldb used by tests, h2 used by the app, plus mysql and postgres for consistency).\n\nBoth new acceptance tests (`coreSoftDeleteFlagsAndRetains`, `coreDeletedOwnerIgnoredByDuplicateCheck`) and the rest of the existing suite pass.",
